<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">I Ching</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th–4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The I Ching, or Book of Changes, began as a Western Zhou divination manual of 64 hexagrams. With the Ten Wings added, it became one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a> in 136 BCE, and it has been read for centuries as divination, moral guidance and philosophy across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and, since the 18th century, in the West.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/i-ching.jpg" alt="I Ching" width="330" height="546" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Song era print artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I_Ching_Song_Dynasty_print.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The core of the I Ching is a divination text called the Changes of Zhou, or Zhou yi. Modern scholars suggest dates between the 10th and 4th centuries BCE for its assembly in roughly its present form. Edward Shaughnessy, comparing its language with dated bronze inscriptions, placed its compilation in the last quarter of the 9th century BCE. A copy among the bamboo slips of the Shanghai Museum, discovered in 1994, shows that the text was used at all levels of Chinese society by 300 BCE. In 136 BCE Emperor Wu of Han named it the first among the classics, the Classic of Changes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The basic unit is the hexagram, a figure of six stacked lines, each broken or unbroken. The received text contains all 64 possible hexagrams, each with a name, a short statement and six line statements. In the canonical order, called the King Wen sequence, hexagrams are paired with their upside-down equivalents. Divination used bundles of yarrow stalks to produce sets of six numbers from 6 to 9, each set matching a hexagram. The canonised book was bound to ten commentaries called the Ten Wings, whose origin academics still call a mystery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Wang Bi in the 3rd century set aside Han numerology and joined the philosophy of the Ten Wings to the text itself. Under the Tang, Emperor Taizong ordered Kong Yingda to produce a canonical edition, which became standard through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. In the 12th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> argued that the book was primarily divination serving moral self-cultivation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</a> wrote the first European commentary in 1703. Richard Wilhelm's German translation of 1923, put into English by Cary Baynes in 1950 with an introduction by Carl Jung, became the most influential in the West.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “I Ching” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Intellectual life was led by aristocrats devoted to qingtan, 'pure conversation' on metaphysical questions drawn from the Laozi, Zhuangzi and <b>Yijing</b>, as recorded in the later Shishuo xinyu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Divination with yarrow stalks, later systematised in the <b>Book of Changes</b>, joined oracle-bone divination.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Yuan dynasty was proclaimed in 1271, when Kublai Khan, grandson of Genghis Khan and Great Khan of the Mongols, adopted the Chinese dynastic name Da Yuan ('Great Origin', a phrase drawn from the <b>Book of Changes</b>) and presented himself as emperor of China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Divination had developed in China by 1250–1150 BCE, and the <b>Yijing</b> and the Eight Trigrams were well established by 1000 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…collection of ancient poems; the Book of Documents, speeches and records of early kings; the Book of Rites, on ceremony and conduct; the <b>Book of Changes</b>, the Yijing, a manual of divination with philosophical commentaries; and the Spring and Autumn Annals, a chronicle of the state of Lu.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Its foundations are the Five Classics, among them the Book of Songs, the oldest collection of Chinese poetry, and the <b>Book of Changes</b>, and the works of the philosophers, among them Confucius, Mencius, Laozi, Zhuangzi, Mozi and Han Feizi, and the Art of War of Sunzi.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
